# Deterministic fuse detector (tools/printcheck fusecheck), run on the SLICED
# STL — never a -D pose. The head prints as TWO bodies: the moving one
# (ball + shank + stud) and the socket assembly (ring + collar + wings + stem
# tenon, one solid joined through the uncut ring below the slit). Like the
# piano hinge there is no flexure web to excuse: a healthy assembly is two
# disjoint shells, counted directly. A ball welded to its socket (clearance
# lost — the failure this design exists to defeat, invisible to printcheck)
# reads as fewer bodies and is a STRONG WARN for reviewer signoff.
assert pip-ball-socket-head-head.stl 2

# The coupon's bodies: four cells, each a socket ring + its free ball
# (the collar cell's slit does not divide the ring — below the slit start the
# ring is solid) = 2 each.
assert pip-ball-socket-head-coupon.stl 8

# Mandatory negative control: part="fused" drives a grown ball into the
# socket, welding the head into one solid. It must STAY one body — proving
# the check can fire (and that no over-generous zone is masking a real fuse
# by splitting the control too).
control fused 1
